exp:
Semaphores are correct but fragile: mutual exclusion depends on every programmer, everywhere, writing wait() and signal() in the right order and never forgetting one. A single reversed pair anywhere in the program breaks the whole system, and the failure is timing-dependent. A monitor moves that responsibility into the language: the compiler generates the entry and exit code, so mutual exclusion cannot be omitted.

Structure:

```
monitor M {
    // shared variables — private to the monitor
    condition x, y;
    procedure P1(...) { ... }
    procedure P2(...) { ... }
    initialization_code(...) { ... }
}
```

Mutual exclusion alone is not enough, so monitors add condition variables. On a condition x:
- `x.wait()` suspends the calling process on x's queue and releases the monitor, so someone else can get in.
- `x.signal()` resumes exactly one process suspended on x. If none is suspended, it has no effect at all.

That last clause is the key contrast with a semaphore. A semaphore signal() always increments and is remembered; a condition-variable signal on an empty queue is lost. Condition variables have no counter.

When P signals and Q was waiting, both could now run inside the monitor. Two resolutions: signal-and-wait (Hoare) — P waits until Q leaves; and signal-and-continue (Mesa) — Q waits until P leaves. Mesa semantics means the condition may be false again by the time Q runs, so Mesa-style code puts `wait()` inside a `while` loop, not an `if`.

With a buffer of n slots, three semaphores are needed:

- `mutex = 1` — mutual exclusion on the buffer.
- `empty = n` — counts free slots.
- `full = 0` — counts filled slots.

Producer:
```
do {
   produce item;
   wait(empty);      // is there room?
   wait(mutex);
      add item to buffer;
   signal(mutex);
   signal(full);     // one more item
} while (true);
```

Consumer:
```
do {
   wait(full);       // is there an item?
   wait(mutex);
      remove item from buffer;
   signal(mutex);
   signal(empty);    // one more free slot
} while (true);
```

The symmetry is the memory hook: the producer waits on `empty` and signals `full`; the consumer waits on `full` and signals `empty`. `empty` and `full` are counting semaphores and always satisfy empty + full ≤ n.

The ordering is not cosmetic. If the producer does `wait(mutex); wait(empty);` instead, and the buffer is full, it holds mutex while blocked on empty. The consumer then blocks on mutex and can never remove an item to release a slot — permanent deadlock. So: **always acquire the counting semaphore before the mutex, and release in the reverse order.**

The unbounded-buffer variant drops `empty` entirely (the producer never blocks) and keeps mutex and full.

The same problem is the standard motivating example for monitors and for pipes.
